The out and outr Directives# The out directive verifies that the output of a code block contains a given string. The outr directive matches using a regular expression.
Basic Usage (out)# The simplest form checks if the output contains a substring:
echo &#34;Hello World&#34;ls /Regex Matching (outr)# Use outr when you need regular expression patterns:
echo &#34;exact&#34;echo &#34;file123.txt&#34;Case Sensitivity# Patterns are case-sensitive by default:
echo &#34;Linux&#34;Quantifiers# Common regex quantifiers work with outr: * (zero or more), + (one or more), ? (zero or one):
echo &#34;goooal&#34;echo &#34;color&#34;Alternation# Use | for OR patterns:
echo &#34;cat&#34;Multiline Output# printf &#34;line1\nline2\nline3&#34;Empty Output# Match empty output with ^$:
trueNegation# Use !out or !outr to assert the output does not contain a pattern:
echo &#34;hello&#34;echo &#34;123&#34;

The cmd Directive# The cmd directive runs a verification command after the code block executes. The test passes if the verification command exits with code 0.
Basic File Checking# Verify a file exists after creation:
touch /tmp/makedo_doc_cmd_test.txtVerify a file was deleted:
rm -f /tmp/makedo_doc_cmd_test.txtDirectory Checking# mkdir -p /tmp/makedo_doc_cmd_dirrmdir /tmp/makedo_doc_cmd_dirTool Availability# Check if a command is installed:
echo &#34;Checking for git...&#34;echo &#34;Checking for bash...&#34;File Content Verification# Use grep to verify file contents:
echo &#34;success&#34; &gt; /tmp/makedo_doc_content.txtVerify a file does NOT contain specific text:
echo &#34;pass&#34; &gt; /tmp/makedo_doc_content2.txtCleanup# rm -f /tmp/makedo_doc_content.txt /tmp/makedo_doc_content2.txtNegation# Use !cmd to assert a command fails:
echo &#34;testing negated cmd&#34;

The pwd Directive# The pwd directive asserts that the current working directory matches a given pattern. It supports a single * wildcard at the start or end of the pattern.
Suffix Match# Match the end of the current directory path:
echo &#34;checking pwd ends with makedo&#34;Prefix Match# Use a wildcard at the end to match the beginning of the path:
echo &#34;checking pwd starts with /home&#34;Negation# Use !pwd to assert you are NOT in a specific directory:
echo &#34;not in /etc&#34;

Environment &amp; State# MakeDo provides mechanisms for managing state across code blocks despite each block running in an isolated shell.
Shell Isolation# Every code block is executed in a new, independent shell process. Variables or functions defined in one block do not persist to the next:
MY_VAR=&#34;defined_here&#34; echo &#34;MY_VAR is set to: $MY_VAR&#34;echo &#34;MY_VAR is now: ${MY_VAR:-empty}&#34;Sharing State with $MAKEDO_ENV# To share variables between blocks, append exports to the $MAKEDO_ENV file. This file is automatically sourced at the beginning of all subsequent code blocks:
echo &#34;export SHARED_VAR=hello_world&#34; &gt;&gt; $MAKEDO_ENVNow SHARED_VAR is available in the next block:
echo &#34;SHARED_VAR is $SHARED_VAR&#34; **Note:** Changes to `$MAKEDO_ENV` take effect in the *next* code block, not the current one. Changing Directories# You can also use $MAKEDO_ENV to change the working directory for subsequent blocks:
mkdir -p /tmp/makedo_doc_env_dir echo &#34;cd /tmp/makedo_doc_env_dir&#34; &gt;&gt; $MAKEDO_ENVtouch testfile.txtInstant Changes# If you need changes immediately in the same block, source $MAKEDO_ENV manually:
echo &#34;export INSTANT_VAR=now&#34; &gt;&gt; $MAKEDO_ENV source $MAKEDO_ENV echo &#34;INSTANT_VAR is $INSTANT_VAR&#34;Disk Persistence# While environment variables require $MAKEDO_ENV, filesystem changes persist across blocks automatically:
echo &#34;persisted&#34; &gt; /tmp/makedo_doc_persist.txtcat /tmp/makedo_doc_persist.txtCleanup# rm -rf /tmp/makedo_doc_env_dir /tmp/makedo_doc_persist.txt

The skip Directive# The skip directive tells MakeDo to completely ignore a code block. The block will not be executed, tested, or embedded. It remains as regular documentation-only content.
Usage# Add &lt;!-- skip --&gt; after any code block to prevent MakeDo from processing it:
echo &#34;This code block will NOT be executed by MakeDo&#34;This is useful for:
Showing example syntax that should not be run Documenting commands that require specific environments Including pseudo-code or template commands Skip Wins Over Other Directives# If skip appears alongside other directives, the block is still skipped entirely. The skip directive always takes priority.
Visible Skip# You can wrap the skip directive in backticks to make it visible in rendered Markdown:
echo &#34;This is also skipped&#34;&lt;!-- skip --&gt;
